00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:50.120
This is, so for me, this is very much

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:54.420
an approach of I'm just talking to you

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:55.480
in the way that I would,

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:56.710
if we weren't recording this,

00:22:56.710 --> 00:22:58.010
like every other podcast.

00:22:58.010 --> 00:23:03.010
Because this is a helpful exercise for me,

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:07.780
but it's not for anybody else.

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:11.410
And it's not to prove
something to myself either.

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:12.243
Mm-hmm.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.250
So, the decision to just listen

00:23:16.250 --> 00:23:18.430
to some 80s country music

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:23.260
was an exercise in freedom for me to say,

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:25.439
I'm not gonna do something

00:23:25.439 --> 00:23:27.840
because I feel like I ought to.

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:31.520
I'm going to do something
because I want to.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:32.460
That is no...

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:35.723
There's nothing wrong with
listen, enjoying music.

00:23:35.723 --> 00:23:38.470
It's like I would get in a place

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:40.760
where I would feel guilty for like,

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:43.240
well, you thought you
were gonna take this time.

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:44.100
Mm-hmm.

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:47.430
To read this book, or
to listen to this book

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:49.640
so that you could gain more insight

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:51.120
and that you could have...

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.360
You could make spiritual progress,

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:56.040
even if you didn't wanna
share it with anybody.

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:57.530
So, you're kind of failing

00:23:57.530 --> 00:23:58.860
on your commitment to yourself.

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:01.990
It's like, I can very easily get in

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:03.830
that head space again,

00:24:03.830 --> 00:24:06.393
because I live there so much.

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:10.250
So, actually the spiritual exercise for me

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:11.923
was listening to Randy Travis.

00:24:11.923 --> 00:24:14.223
Well, it's pretty damn spiritual.

00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:16.130
Well, it's so good.

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:18.410
I didn't listen to any
of his gospel songs.

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:20.010
So, it was, but yeah,

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:25.010
it's opening up my
definition of spirituality.

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:28.599
And I think, yes, I think,

00:24:28.599 --> 00:24:30.920
it's kind of a joke to say

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:32.730
that listening to Randy Travis

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:35.023
is a spiritual exercise.

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:41.670
But I do think legitimately

00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:46.090
it is a step in my personal growth

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:48.773
and self-acceptance, self compassion.

00:24:49.660 --> 00:24:53.840
So that that positions me for more growth

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:57.230
in every way, including
spiritually down the road,

00:24:57.230 --> 00:24:58.063
to come to grips with these things

00:24:58.063 --> 00:24:59.323
that I'm talking about.

00:25:00.780 --> 00:25:03.333
Well, you know, one of the things

00:25:03.333 --> 00:25:04.420
I was actually talking about with my...

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:05.930
Talking with my brother about this,

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:06.990
'cause while you were there,

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:08.400
I was in North Carolina.

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:09.960
Mm-hmm.

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:14.730
And the...

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:20.650
The idea that...

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:23.670
'Cause I was telling him about this,

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:26.240
about the book the "Before
You Lose Your Faith."

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:29.120
Yeah, deconstructing doubt in the church.

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:32.340
And there is this, you know,

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:35.020
in the evangelical church,

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:39.210
and really in sort of
traditional Christianity,

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:42.007
just like an Orthodox
Christianity worldview.

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:46.820
There is an idea that

00:25:46.820 --> 00:25:48.340
you can't trust yourself, right?

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:52.510
That you you're fallen, you're sinful

00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:56.000
and your instincts are ultimately bad,

00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:57.140
because you're corrupted

00:25:57.140 --> 00:25:58.880
because of original sin.

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:02.070
And the only way to have pure desires

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:05.380
and you gotta get that
from the holy spirit.

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:06.980
You gotta get that from God, right?

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:09.570
And so, that's the way of thinking

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:12.981
that we were marinated in.

00:26:12.981 --> 00:26:13.814
Yeah.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:16.730
And so, I see a lot of that in this book

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:18.090
as it talks about like,

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:20.300
when you start following your instincts

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:23.750
that is perceived as a bad thing by

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:24.583
a lot of Christians.

00:26:24.583 --> 00:26:26.140
Because they're like,
"Well, your instincts

00:26:26.140 --> 00:26:27.360
are gonna be misguided."

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:29.180
Your understanding is
always gonna fall short,

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:30.940
it's God's understanding and God's insight

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:32.763
that you have to kind of lean on.

00:26:34.112 --> 00:26:35.162
And it kind of hit me

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:37.900
because one of the biggest things that

00:26:37.900 --> 00:26:41.630
has happened post deconstruction

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:45.333
is learning to trust my own intuition.

00:26:45.333 --> 00:26:48.060
It's something my wife talks about a lot,

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:49.390
which is even more difficult

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:51.960
for a woman who is brought up as someone

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:53.070
who's supposed to be submissive

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:54.409
to the man in her life, right?

00:26:54.409 --> 00:26:55.242
Mm-hmm, mm-hmm.

00:26:55.242 --> 00:26:57.040
So, not only can you not trust yourself,

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:59.350
but you gotta trust the man in your life,

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:00.320
and you gotta trust God

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:02.430
before you trust yourself.

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:05.780
And you lose the ability, a lot of times,

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:07.290
you come out of that and you have

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:08.900
the inability to decipher

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:12.110
between desire and intuition.

00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:15.570
And this is relevant
to what you're saying.

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:16.403
Yeah, yeah.

00:27:16.403 --> 00:27:17.727
Because you act...

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:24.920
Most people who have an
intact moral compass,

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:27.500
which I believe is the
vast majority of people.

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:29.190
There are people who have certain things,

00:27:29.190 --> 00:27:30.570
whether it's environmental, or genetic,

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:34.300
that maybe they're just
kind of bad eggs, right?

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:35.133
And they're gonna grow up

00:27:35.133 --> 00:27:37.280
and be serial killers, or whatever.

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:41.390
But most people know
what's right and wrong

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:44.380
in a general sense.

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:45.213
And that doesn't...

00:27:45.213 --> 00:27:46.130
So, that doesn't mean

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:47.410
you just follow your desires,

00:27:47.410 --> 00:27:49.710
'cause if my desire is
to do something hurtful

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:52.393
to you, or to my wife,
or my kids, or whatever,

00:27:53.430 --> 00:27:55.720
or to do something that would be hurtful

00:27:55.720 --> 00:27:57.590
to the people in my life.

00:27:57.590 --> 00:27:59.350
I might still desire to do it,

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:03.610
but my intuition, 99%
of the time tells me,

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:05.310
well, you know that would be wrong.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.120
That would be a selfish decision.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:09.610
You can actually trust
that intuition, right?

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:11.430
So, when you talk,

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:12.650
and we're talking about the definition

00:28:12.650 --> 00:28:14.810
of spirituality being much more broad

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:16.460
than we ever understood it to be.

00:28:16.460 --> 00:28:17.900
So, when you talk about listening

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:19.440
to Randy Travis, making the decision

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:23.210
to listen to Randy
Travis, or not listen...

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:24.715
Not listening to a book

00:28:24.715 --> 00:28:25.970
that would be quote unquote, spiritual

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:27.220
in the traditional sense.

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:30.830
I think that both of us

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:32.520
and lots of deconstructing Christians

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:35.500
are learning to trust your own intuition

00:28:35.500 --> 00:28:38.980
and believing that you actually do know

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:40.600
what's best for yourself.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:42.410
Yeah.
And in a given moment.

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:45.130
So, 'cause as it relates to

00:28:45.130 --> 00:28:47.740
I'm taking a different
path right now, right?

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:50.660
Like, I mean, yes, I love Randy Travis

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:52.079
and I can see how listening

00:28:52.079 --> 00:28:53.679
to him would be a spiritual exercise.

00:28:53.679 --> 00:28:57.330
But this morning, yeah, I'm working out,

00:28:57.330 --> 00:28:58.760
my wife comes down to the garage

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:00.587
and I'm listening to "Figuring,"

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:04.640
Maria Popova's very long book.

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:06.070
And it's like, she's talking

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:07.220
about some esoteric thing,

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:09.390
where I'm sitting there
doing shoulder exercises.

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:10.223
And she didn't...

00:29:10.223 --> 00:29:12.050
Jesse just laughs out loud.

00:29:12.050 --> 00:29:12.883
She's like-
Yeah.

00:29:12.883 --> 00:29:14.330
"I don't understand," cause she,

00:29:14.330 --> 00:29:16.630
'cause when Jesse comes in the gym,

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:18.480
she wants to turn on Lizzo, or whatever

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:19.920
and get pumped up.
Hell, yeah!

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:21.438
She's like, "How do
you not get pumped up?"

00:29:21.438 --> 00:29:22.830
I was like, "Because that's not

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:23.780
how I get pumped up."

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:27.070
It's like, I don't do this,

00:29:27.070 --> 00:29:29.780
I'm not reading the Bible
to please someone else.

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:31.240
I'm not reading the Bible

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:33.250
because there's some obligation.

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:34.420
I'm reading the Bible

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:37.770
because I'm interested
in it and I'm following

00:29:37.770 --> 00:29:39.860
my intuition towards the thing.

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:41.190
And when you made a decision

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:42.510
to listen to Randy Travis,

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:44.170
you're doing the same thing.

00:29:44.170 --> 00:29:46.100
Just because it's a different application,

00:29:46.100 --> 00:29:48.070
or a different path, doesn't make

00:29:48.070 --> 00:29:49.647
it less spiritual.

00:29:49.647 --> 00:29:54.460
The old you thinks that
it's less spiritual.

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:56.100
And that because you can hear that voice.

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:57.720
And that per that voice is still there.

00:29:57.720 --> 00:30:00.083
Let me illustrate in a couple of ways.

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:03.810
I wanted, since we were shouted out

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:06.260
in the opening of the introduction,

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:07.790
of "Before You Lose Your Faith,"

00:30:07.790 --> 00:30:08.970
I did wanna read some of it.

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:12.380
And I went through the table of contents

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:13.550
to see what I should read,

00:30:13.550 --> 00:30:14.960
'cause I didn't think I was gonna read

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:16.610
the whole thing.

00:30:16.610 --> 00:30:18.380
And I skipped all the way

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:19.830
through part one, part two.

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:23.580
I get to the very end and there was

00:30:23.580 --> 00:30:25.370
a chapter called...

00:30:25.370 --> 00:30:27.036
The next to last chapter is called,

00:30:27.036 --> 00:30:29.460
"Sometimes People Don't Believe."

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:30.293
Mm-hmm.

00:30:30.293 --> 00:30:33.203
And I'm like, oh, okay,
this one's about me.

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:36.120
This one's about me now.

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:37.950
So, I'm gonna read this one.

00:30:37.950 --> 00:30:39.980
So, I'm gonna skip all the way to the end.

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:44.057
And I started reading
Jared Wilson's essay,

00:30:44.057 --> 00:30:46.190
"Sometimes People Don't Believe,"

00:30:46.190 --> 00:30:51.190
and it kind of...

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.393
It's kind of written like a sermon,

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:57.940
because it was such a flashback for me.

00:30:57.940 --> 00:30:59.350
It's like I could see him

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:02.933
because of his writing
style and the words,

00:31:02.933 --> 00:31:05.120
and the principles that he's sharing,

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:06.320
and like reading scripture.

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:09.880
It's just like, it was
like I was back in church.

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:11.106
Mm-hmm.

00:31:11.106 --> 00:31:14.060
Hearing him, he writes like he talks,

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:17.090
like he preaches, I kind of inferred.

00:31:17.090 --> 00:31:21.910
And it was, I just noticed

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:25.450
that I just started to feel kind of sick

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:30.640
and it didn't get...

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:32.590
I didn't get any insight into it besides

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:35.670
the fact that I realized that

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:38.010
it was actually triggering, like...

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:39.177
Mm-hmm.

00:31:39.177 --> 00:31:41.370
I mean, you hear the term

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:44.170
and you throw it around a lot.

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:48.680
Honestly, I've always
felt like I'm not a person

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:49.523
to be triggered.

00:31:51.130 --> 00:31:52.870
I'm ashamed to admit

00:31:52.870 --> 00:31:54.430
that I've always thought that

00:31:54.430 --> 00:31:56.444
that word applies to other people.

00:31:56.444 --> 00:31:58.033
Mm-hmm.

00:31:58.033 --> 00:32:00.270
That you've really gotta be,

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:01.490
you've gotta have...

00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:04.940
You've gotta have some specific trauma

00:32:04.940 --> 00:32:06.290
in order for that to happen

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:12.330
and I just don't go to church.

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:14.790
I don't listen to sermons anymore,

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:16.510
and I don't read the Bible anymore.

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:19.436
So, I kind of have stayed away from that,

00:32:19.436 --> 00:32:23.200
like crossing the threshold of a church

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:25.900
in any form, in any sensation.

00:32:25.900 --> 00:32:27.940
So, to find myself there by reading this

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:30.970
it was also a surprise to me

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:34.108
that it just kind of felt like...

00:32:34.108 --> 00:32:38.550
I mean, the specifics of the chapter were,

00:32:38.550 --> 00:32:41.050
it was kind of like this more

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:44.600
the strawman deconstruction.

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:46.450
And it was just an oversimplification

00:32:46.450 --> 00:32:51.050
and left and right, it
was a dismissal that I...

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:53.150
And because I felt like

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:55.190
they were talking about some...

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:56.200
They really weren't talking

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:57.740
to someone who doesn't believe,

00:32:57.740 --> 00:33:00.913
they were talking to people who might,

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:04.060
who might stop believing,

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:05.360
which is the whole point of the book.

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:06.960
Or really, he started talking

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:09.210
to people who were frustrated

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:10.770
and beating themselves up

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:13.590
because their evangelism
wasn't effective enough.

00:33:13.590 --> 00:33:15.230
That's actually what a lot of the chapter-

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:16.210
Yeah.
Ended up being about.

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:17.932
Mm-hmm.
And there was a...

00:33:17.932 --> 00:33:19.540
That was a big flashback to when

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:24.500
all of our training in evangelism,

00:33:24.500 --> 00:33:28.300
it was just heaps and heaps of obligation.

00:33:28.300 --> 00:33:32.980
That then became shame whenever

00:33:32.980 --> 00:33:34.480
we couldn't seal the deal,

00:33:34.480 --> 00:33:37.430
or go out on the beach

00:33:37.430 --> 00:33:39.830
and save some stranger, you know?

00:33:39.830 --> 00:33:41.940
Even though you do everything you can

00:33:41.940 --> 00:33:43.471
and you leave the results to God.

00:33:43.471 --> 00:33:46.180
We always taught that, but again,

00:33:46.180 --> 00:33:48.050
it just reminded me in almost

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:51.980
like a physical sense of that feeling

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:56.980
of not being good enough.

00:33:57.200 --> 00:34:01.020
Not ever being able to
cut it, to live up to it.

00:34:01.020 --> 00:34:02.220
You didn't fully embrace

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:04.733
the reformed tradition
that you were a part of.

00:34:05.730 --> 00:34:06.860
And then-
Because if you had,

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:08.070
you would've been like-
Yeah.

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:11.026
I do my part and then God does

00:34:11.026 --> 00:34:12.590
the important part.

00:34:12.590 --> 00:34:13.423
Yeah.

00:34:13.423 --> 00:34:14.256
And that's all I can do.

00:34:14.256 --> 00:34:16.680
I would say that I
believed that and that...

00:34:16.680 --> 00:34:17.700
I did believe that.

00:34:17.700 --> 00:34:21.250
So, I guess I didn't have as much strife

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:22.720
about it because I'd be like, well,

00:34:22.720 --> 00:34:24.300
if God doesn't regenerate this person,

00:34:24.300 --> 00:34:27.630
then that's God's issue.

00:34:27.630 --> 00:34:30.160
Ultimately that's where
he goes in this chapter

00:34:30.160 --> 00:34:31.910
is talking about how

00:34:31.910 --> 00:34:36.110
it's a spiritual response that can...

00:34:36.110 --> 00:34:38.260
A conversion is a spiritual response

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:39.493
that can only be,

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:44.290
it originates with God.

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:46.743
So, if you don't,

00:34:48.202 --> 00:34:50.720
if someone that you care about,

00:34:50.720 --> 00:34:52.230
or a stranger on the beach,

00:34:52.230 --> 00:34:55.300
or anyone in between doesn't respond

00:34:55.300 --> 00:34:56.870
and they just don't believe.

00:34:56.870 --> 00:34:59.180
It's, well, some people just don't believe

00:34:59.180 --> 00:35:04.060
because they're not
chosen by God to believe,

00:35:04.060 --> 00:35:06.510
'cause if God really wants...

00:35:06.510 --> 00:35:08.190
'Cause he ends up describing,

00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:10.280
the way that he ends it is,

00:35:10.280 --> 00:35:12.530
if you're really concerned
that you might not

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:14.180
have a saving faith,

00:35:14.180 --> 00:35:15.500
this is how the whole chapter ends.

00:35:15.500 --> 00:35:17.810
It's like, well, if
you're concerned about it,

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:18.940
that means that God has made

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:19.773
you concerned about it.

00:35:19.773 --> 00:35:20.790
So, you don't need to be concerned

00:35:20.790 --> 00:35:21.623
about it anymore.

00:35:21.623 --> 00:35:22.814
You're definitely safe.

00:35:22.814 --> 00:35:23.750
That's a sign that you're safe, right?

00:35:23.750 --> 00:35:26.164
I actually never struggled

00:35:26.164 --> 00:35:29.550
with was I actually saved?

00:35:29.550 --> 00:35:31.310
Because I felt like struggling

00:35:31.310 --> 00:35:35.853
with that meant my faith
was not strong enough.

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:38.263
Hmm.

00:35:38.263 --> 00:35:40.620
So, I would never admit to myself

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:43.450
that I ever questioned whether I was saved

00:35:43.450 --> 00:35:48.450
because a strong faith
would never do that.

00:35:50.660 --> 00:35:53.117
So, it was just my way of thinking

00:35:53.117 --> 00:35:54.420
and the way that I interacted

00:35:54.420 --> 00:35:55.713
with the whole system.

00:35:57.850 --> 00:35:59.390
I feel like I'm willing to admit,

00:35:59.390 --> 00:36:00.890
and every time I talk about this,

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:03.770
I go back to this point of like,

00:36:03.770 --> 00:36:05.790
yeah, a lot of what I
brought to the table,

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:08.670
like my experience, my
genetics, my environment.

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:12.283
Everything about my past builds

00:36:15.150 --> 00:36:19.370
me to react to this evangelical system

00:36:19.370 --> 00:36:22.110
in a way that regardless of how much

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:24.880
you talk about grace and love,

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:26.720
I'm still gonna beat myself up.

00:36:26.720 --> 00:36:28.300
That's just a fact of the matter.

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:30.980
That's exactly how it went.

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:32.870
And then, it was just a reminder

00:36:32.870 --> 00:36:34.737
that this totally brought it all back

00:36:34.737 --> 00:36:36.210
and triggered all of it.

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:37.550
Well, one of the things

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:39.310
that makes that difficult for...

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:42.290
'Cause my wife is...

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:44.000
She's a different
personality than you, but-

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:45.560
But we relate a lot in this way.

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:46.550
But she is-

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:47.450
As we've talked.

00:36:48.371 --> 00:36:51.150
She suffers from obsessive
compulsive disorder

00:36:51.150 --> 00:36:52.693
and specifically scrupulosity,

00:36:54.441 --> 00:36:56.230
which is a type of OCD.

00:36:56.230 --> 00:36:59.600
In which if you're a religious person,

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:01.530
a person of faith, it can kind of turn

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:02.440
it into overdrive.

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:03.650
Where you're basically trying to be

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:07.160
the best possible
Christian that you can be.

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:09.150
And it's interesting when you're in a...

00:37:09.150 --> 00:37:11.270
Especially, she was raised in-

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:13.250
You may not be washing your hands

00:37:14.470 --> 00:37:15.472
to the point of detriment.
She kind of does that,

00:37:15.472 --> 00:37:16.982
she does that a little bit to, but-

00:37:16.982 --> 00:37:17.815
But you're kind of,

00:37:17.815 --> 00:37:18.950
you feel like you gotta wash your spirit.

00:37:18.950 --> 00:37:19.783
Yeah.

00:37:19.783 --> 00:37:20.858
Your conscience.

00:37:20.858 --> 00:37:21.691
And she-
Constantly.

00:37:21.691 --> 00:37:26.440
She went to a Baptist Christian school

00:37:26.440 --> 00:37:27.337
where they said things like,

00:37:27.337 --> 00:37:29.810
"If you're 99.9% sure that you're saved,

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:31.540
you're 100% lost."

00:37:31.540 --> 00:37:32.650
Now, they would never have said that

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:34.640
at a reformed institution,

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:35.780
which we ended up going

00:37:35.780 --> 00:37:37.730
to essentially, a reformed church, right?

00:37:37.730 --> 00:37:38.640
Mm-hmm.

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:41.200
And so, that helped somewhat,

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:43.340
but the thing is that the Bible

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:45.410
and also, just Christian teaching

00:37:45.410 --> 00:37:47.870
and Christian culture in general,

00:37:47.870 --> 00:37:51.160
the idea of grace through faith

00:37:51.160 --> 00:37:52.737
and it being a gift from God,

00:37:52.737 --> 00:37:53.570
and this being,

00:37:53.570 --> 00:37:56.010
basically 100% God's responsibility,

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:57.580
and you're just basically responding.

00:37:57.580 --> 00:37:58.660
You're actually, you're not,

00:37:58.660 --> 00:37:59.710
if you're truly reformed

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:00.740
you're not even responding.

00:38:00.740 --> 00:38:02.763
Your response is determined by God.

00:38:05.740 --> 00:38:07.430
It actually, doesn't quite jive

00:38:07.430 --> 00:38:11.080
with the idea of all the responsibilities,

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:12.500
'cause if you just look at what Jesus had

00:38:12.500 --> 00:38:13.700
to say, Jesus is talking about

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:15.710
a lot of stuff that we should be doing.

00:38:15.710 --> 00:38:17.510
Not everybody who says, "Lord, Lord,"

00:38:17.510 --> 00:38:19.260
will be saved, but only those who do

00:38:19.260 --> 00:38:20.657
the will of my father.

00:38:20.657 --> 00:38:22.310
The reformed tradition takes that

00:38:22.310 --> 00:38:24.360
and says, oh, this means that it's not

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:26.220
about works, when another way to read

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:27.053
that is Jesus saying,

00:38:27.053 --> 00:38:28.230
"No, actually it kind of,

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:29.823
what you do is sort of important."

00:38:29.823 --> 00:38:30.984
You know-
But then-

00:38:30.984 --> 00:38:34.110
So, regardless of your theology,

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:36.200
your personality is gonna latch onto

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:37.760
the obligation and the shame

00:38:37.760 --> 00:38:39.337
that sort of permeates
the whole way of thinking.

00:38:39.337 --> 00:38:40.570
And by the way, there's a...

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:43.450
There's at least part of what I'm saying

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:45.290
that is kind of like, you know what?

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:46.203
It's my fault.

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:49.253
And so, I hear myself saying that

00:38:49.253 --> 00:38:50.950
and I'm like, you know what?

00:38:50.950 --> 00:38:53.733
I don't know if I wanna just accept that.

00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:56.246
So, 'cause I don't...

00:38:56.246 --> 00:38:59.130
I'm not comfortable placing blame

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:01.870
and talking bad about anybody,

00:39:01.870 --> 00:39:05.140
or the church and coming after anybody

00:39:05.140 --> 00:39:09.900
when my tendency always is to say,

00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:12.230
okay, yeah, this is on me.

00:39:12.230 --> 00:39:13.063
This is on me.

00:39:14.715 --> 00:39:19.010
And these are the things
that I'm deconstructing

00:39:19.010 --> 00:39:20.970
and a lot of it is belief by belief.

00:39:20.970 --> 00:39:23.080
Like very early on,

00:39:23.080 --> 00:39:26.270
it was deconstructing the belief

00:39:26.270 --> 00:39:28.713
that if I left the church,

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:30.833
even if Christie didn't,

00:39:32.210 --> 00:39:34.180
our marriage would not survive.

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:35.190
We've already talked about that.

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:36.023
Right.

00:39:37.110 --> 00:39:42.110
But recently, the teaching

00:39:42.370 --> 00:39:43.910
has been ringing in my ears

00:39:43.910 --> 00:39:45.580
that where Jesus said,

00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:48.520
it's easier for a camel to go through

00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:52.130
the eye of a needle than for what?

00:39:52.130 --> 00:39:53.483
A rich man to enter the kingdom.

00:39:53.483 --> 00:39:56.598
A rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven,

00:39:56.598 --> 00:39:58.570
'cause I, on the lats episode.

00:39:58.570 --> 00:39:59.690
Whoa, whoa, you're rich?

00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:00.880
Are you on the Forbes list?

00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:01.720
I'm on the Forbes list.

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:03.530
Are you above or below Ryan,

00:40:03.530 --> 00:40:04.363
the nine year old?

00:40:04.363 --> 00:40:05.450
I'm below him.

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:06.510
Okay.

00:40:06.510 --> 00:40:07.343
But he-
He might be 10 now.

00:40:07.343 --> 00:40:08.302
Right.
Though, so it's-

00:40:08.302 --> 00:40:09.320
I think, we're above him now,

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:10.870
but there's another kid above us.

00:40:10.870 --> 00:40:12.010
Oh, crap.

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:13.265
Yeah, it's probably...

00:40:13.265 --> 00:40:14.960
I think it's a younger girl.

00:40:14.960 --> 00:40:17.630
How easy is it for a rich kid to enter

00:40:17.630 --> 00:40:18.840
the kingdom of God?

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:21.167
A kid can go through the eye

00:40:21.167 --> 00:40:22.000
of a needle easier than a-

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:22.840
They're smaller.
Easier than a camel.

00:40:22.840 --> 00:40:23.750
The older you get, though,

00:40:23.750 --> 00:40:24.710
the bigger you get, typically.

00:40:24.710 --> 00:40:25.543
Yeah, yeah.

00:40:25.543 --> 00:40:27.130
Unless you're Benjamin Button.

00:40:27.130 --> 00:40:28.180
Benjamin Button can get right through

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:29.201
that damn needle.

00:40:29.201 --> 00:40:30.314
Eventually.

00:40:30.314 --> 00:40:32.170
Yeah, it takes a while, though.

00:40:32.170 --> 00:40:34.150
You talked about privilege,

00:40:34.150 --> 00:40:39.150
the privilege that we
have, and so when I say...

00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:42.920
Last week, so when I say
things like, I'm happy.

00:40:42.920 --> 00:40:43.840
And you're rich.

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:47.120
And it's like, well,
is it because I'm rich?

00:40:47.120 --> 00:40:50.900
Am I blinded to like,

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:54.310
'cause I believed that, yeah, what?

00:40:54.310 --> 00:40:55.910
I believed the teaching it's like,

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:57.770
if you're rich, you got...

00:40:57.770 --> 00:40:59.913
You think you've got everything you want,

00:41:01.250 --> 00:41:04.523
so you're missing out
on what you really need.

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:07.040
Not only salvation,

00:41:07.040 --> 00:41:12.040
but like an active dynamic relationship

00:41:12.310 --> 00:41:14.790
with God through Jesus.

00:41:14.790 --> 00:41:18.490
That's what we were taught to believe,

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:21.306
and so I've found myself even wrestling

00:41:21.306 --> 00:41:23.500
with that over the past couple of weeks

00:41:23.500 --> 00:41:24.970
that like, is the...

00:41:24.970 --> 00:41:27.380
Am I lying to myself

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:29.980
just because I'm well off?

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:33.663
Is it easy for me to be
happy for that reason?

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:39.300
So, it's a belief that's always been there

00:41:39.300 --> 00:41:41.960
that I have to pick up
and I have to look at,

00:41:41.960 --> 00:41:45.740
and I have to dismantle it.

00:41:45.740 --> 00:41:49.290
Or see if maybe I'm wrong about it,

00:41:49.290 --> 00:41:53.110
because I hear that voice saying...

00:41:53.110 --> 00:41:54.713
My former self.

00:41:56.614 --> 00:41:58.890
Okay, maybe it's not my formal self,

00:41:58.890 --> 00:42:00.520
maybe it's Jesus.

00:42:00.520 --> 00:42:02.320
Maybe it's the Jesus

00:42:02.320 --> 00:42:05.840
that I had a relationship with.

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:08.790
That's what I always heard from him.

00:42:08.790 --> 00:42:10.610
It's kind of like you don't hear

00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:12.230
the thousands of positive comments,

00:42:12.230 --> 00:42:13.760
you only hear the negative one.

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:15.424
It's a human tendency.
Oh, we don't get...

00:42:15.424 --> 00:42:17.249
We don't get those.

00:42:17.249 --> 00:42:18.348
Do we?

00:42:18.348 --> 00:42:21.270
Even in this chapter,

00:42:21.270 --> 00:42:23.290
he talked about something else

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:28.290
that kind of, I guess,
triggered this again.

00:42:29.910 --> 00:42:33.890
When Jesus is telling
the story of the rich man

00:42:33.890 --> 00:42:36.840
who's burning in hell, okay,

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:38.760
this is Luke 16.

00:42:38.760 --> 00:42:39.780
If you're following along

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:44.780
in your Rhett study Bible,
Luke 16:19 through 31.

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:50.290
So, the rich man's burning in hell,

00:42:50.290 --> 00:42:52.440
but he's looking across the chasm

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:56.270
at Abraham, and he's able to talk to him.

00:42:56.270 --> 00:42:57.727
Is he in the bosom?

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:00.210
The bosom's not mentioned here.

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:01.787
The rich man said,

00:43:01.787 --> 00:43:04.410
"Then I beg you, father,"
talking to Abraham,

00:43:04.410 --> 00:43:09.410
to send him, yeah, talking about Lazarus.

00:43:10.360 --> 00:43:12.000
Like another guy who's like...

00:43:13.700 --> 00:43:15.580
Send him to my father's house,

00:43:15.580 --> 00:43:17.680
where I got five brothers, who are living.

00:43:19.030 --> 00:43:20.500
So that he may warn them, less

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:22.733
they also coming to this place of torment.

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:25.040
It's like, I got five brothers,

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:27.500
just raise him from the dead,

00:43:27.500 --> 00:43:29.070
send him over there.

00:43:29.070 --> 00:43:31.160
Tell him that I'm burning in hell,

00:43:31.160 --> 00:43:32.610
they don't need to come here.

00:43:34.550 --> 00:43:36.120
This is the rich man.

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:37.320
You see what I'm saying?

00:43:39.810 --> 00:43:44.377
And he said, but Abraham said,

00:43:44.377 --> 00:43:46.540
"They Moses and the prophets,

00:43:46.540 --> 00:43:47.600
let them hear them."

00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:49.773
So, they have all the truth they need.

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:54.550
And he said, "No, if someone goes to them

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:57.120
from the dead, they will repent."

00:43:57.120 --> 00:43:58.580
They'll change their mind,

00:43:58.580 --> 00:44:00.927
and then he said, Abraham said,

00:44:00.927 --> 00:44:02.720
"If they do not hear
Moses and the prophets,

00:44:02.720 --> 00:44:03.890
neither will they be convinced

00:44:03.890 --> 00:44:05.860
if someone should rise from the dead."

00:44:05.860 --> 00:44:07.530
Of course, this is Jesus talking,

00:44:07.530 --> 00:44:09.700
referring to himself.

00:44:09.700 --> 00:44:12.207
So, it's, you know,

00:44:12.207 --> 00:44:15.500
and it's the first thing that I feel,

00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:17.520
even reading this two days ago

00:44:17.520 --> 00:44:19.410
is still all of that's...

00:44:19.410 --> 00:44:21.560
All of that teaching just
Wells up inside of me

00:44:21.560 --> 00:44:24.510
that's like, yeah, you
gonna be burning in hell.

00:44:24.510 --> 00:44:25.680
You don't wanna even believe in hell,

00:44:25.680 --> 00:44:27.340
but you're gonna be burning in it one day.

00:44:27.340 --> 00:44:28.767
And you're gonna...

00:44:28.767 --> 00:44:32.860
Because you're a rich
dude, you lied to yourself.

00:44:32.860 --> 00:44:34.720
It's like, I don't...

00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:37.610
I don't lay awake at night
thinking these things,

00:44:37.610 --> 00:44:39.460
but that's why I'm saying,

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:40.890
I was triggered in so many ways

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:42.140
because it brings up all

00:44:42.140 --> 00:44:43.840
these things that like...

00:44:49.110 --> 00:44:49.943
These lies.

00:44:50.790 --> 00:44:53.540
You think about the whole idea

00:44:53.540 --> 00:44:55.100
of being rich, right?

00:44:55.100 --> 00:44:58.550
It's like, we actually can quantify

00:44:58.550 --> 00:45:02.100
how wealth directly influences happiness

00:45:02.100 --> 00:45:04.470
because not from a spiritual standpoint,

00:45:04.470 --> 00:45:05.970
or not from reading the Bible,

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:08.615
but from science, right?

00:45:08.615 --> 00:45:11.720
They've actually done a
lot of research on this

00:45:11.720 --> 00:45:14.530
and they have found that
up to a certain point,

00:45:14.530 --> 00:45:17.840
yes, more money does
bring you more happiness,

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:19.210
but above a certain point-

00:45:19.210 --> 00:45:20.043
Right.

00:45:20.043 --> 00:45:21.590
It levels out.

00:45:21.590 --> 00:45:22.507
And most people have...

00:45:22.507 --> 00:45:24.940
And most people have the ability,

00:45:24.940 --> 00:45:26.900
or regardless of the circumstances,

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:28.180
they have a baseline of happiness

00:45:28.180 --> 00:45:29.130
that they return to once

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:30.820
their circumstances level out.

00:45:30.820 --> 00:45:32.940
Once a transitional period

00:45:32.940 --> 00:45:34.363
stops happening, right?

00:45:35.360 --> 00:45:37.610
And so the fact is, is that you know what?

00:45:37.610 --> 00:45:39.870
The fact that you and I don't have

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:42.923
to worry about is there
going to be enough money?

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:47.420
That is a thing that
took up a lot of space

00:45:47.420 --> 00:45:49.490
in our mind for most of our 20s

00:45:49.490 --> 00:45:50.840
when we were on Staff Of Crusade

00:45:50.840 --> 00:45:51.720
and we're raising support,

00:45:51.720 --> 00:45:52.820
we're asking people for money,

00:45:52.820 --> 00:45:53.680
and we're getting to places.

00:45:53.680 --> 00:45:56.010
Where it's like, I'm not gonna have to ask

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:57.060
my family for money in order

00:45:57.060 --> 00:45:58.350
to pay the grocery bill.

00:45:58.350 --> 00:45:59.700
Like we were in that place

00:45:59.700 --> 00:46:00.700
for a long time, right?

00:46:00.700 --> 00:46:01.880
And for me that-

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:02.713
Your whole childhood.

00:46:02.713 --> 00:46:04.918
That fear goes much deeper

00:46:04.918 --> 00:46:06.260
into my psyche too, so-

00:46:06.260 --> 00:46:08.396
Yeah, so you're not ever gonna lose that.

00:46:08.396 --> 00:46:13.140
To feel relief by not having to worry

00:46:13.140 --> 00:46:16.360
about money in the same way is like,

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:17.990
something that then I'm like,

